<UserControl x:Class="Plith.Views.Widgets.ShelfWidget"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:p="clr-namespace:Plith.Views.Presentation"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             mc:Ignorable="d"
             Focusable="False"
             Background="Transparent">
    <UserControl.Resources>
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                <ResourceDictionary Source="pack://application:,,,/Plith;component/Resources/PlithIcons.xaml" />
            </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!-- What is on the shelf.

         A row of tiles rather than a list of lines, because the shelf answers "what did I put
         here" at a glance, and a name is a slower way to recognise your own file than its shape
         and position.

         No heading. The first pass had one, with a "+3 more" label in the opposite corner, and
         rendering it showed why neither survives: the heading cost 25 of the 76 DIP the page
         has, which clipped every tile label through the middle. The page dots already say which
         page this is, and no other page in the frame introduces itself. What the row cannot show
         is now the last tile instead - a count in a corner is something to read, a tile is
         something that looks like the rest of the row and is understood without being read.

         A Grid now rather than the row alone, because the row gained a line under it. The row's
         track is star-sized and the line's is Auto, so the line cannot be pushed off the page by
         a row that measures taller than it was meant to: the row clips inside its own track
         instead, which is the same rule WidgetFrame applies to the page as a whole.

         The bottom margin is 22, not 24, and the row is 68 tall rather than 76. Those two DIP
         plus the eight are what the line under the row is made of: 116 less 12 and 22 leaves an
         82 DIP content box, the line measures about 13.3, and 68 is what is left.

         Where that has to end is the part worth stating precisely, because the first version of
         this comment got it wrong. WidgetFrame's bottom row is 23 DIP, so its rail LANE begins at
         y=93, not 96 - the page's content ends at about that line. The rail's own 16 DIP target
         is centred in the lane and starts at 96.5, and its visible 3 DIP mark sits at about 101.
         The rail is invisible most of the time and fades in on every page turn, so anything
         overlapping it is a collision that only appears while the page is being changed. -->
    <!-- The row and the line under it are ONE centred block, not a star row with the line
         pinned to the bottom.

         They were two rows, which was right at 116 DIP and wrong at 164: the tiles ended up in
         the middle of the band and the line against the bottom edge, with a hole between them
         that read as a page missing something. Kept together they centre as a pair, and the line
         also moves away from the page rail's lane, which it used to sit just above. -->
    <Grid Margin="{x:Static p:NotchGeometry.PageInsetDip}">
        <!-- The empty state's own host, so its dashed box can STRETCH to the band instead of
             being a fixed height centred by arithmetic. The box that was there carried a 7 DIP
             top margin, which was half the difference between a 68 DIP row and the content box
             of a 116 DIP frame: both of those numbers are gone, and a number derived from one
             frame size does not survive the next one. A box that fills what it is given needs no
             such number. -->
        <Grid x:Name="EmptyHost" Visibility="Collapsed" />

        <StackPanel x:Name="FilledBlock" VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <StackPanel x:Name="Tiles"
                    Orientation="Horizontal"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Center" />

        <!-- The one place the product says the shelf opens.

             Set in XAML rather than built in Render, because it is the same sentence whatever
             the row above it holds, and a line rebuilt on every shelf change is a line that can
             be rebuilt wrongly. Render only chooses whether it is showing and, when the shelf
             cannot open, replaces the text with the reason. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="Hint"
                   Margin="0,10,0,0"
                   Text="Click to open the shelf"
                   FontSize="10"
                   TextAlignment="Center"
                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource NotchInkMuted}"
                   Visibility="Collapsed" />
        </StackPanel>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
